        "type": "thinking",
        "thinking": "I'm deciding to design my own African bush elephant model rather than reuse the existing file, planning proportions like shoulder height around 3.2m, body length, and a rest pose with spread ears, a curved hanging trunk, tusks, and a separate jaw.\n\nI'm now working out detailed coordinates for the body, spine, and limbs—placing spine points along the z-axis with specific radii for the rump, mid-body, and chest to shape the torso, and planning slight variation in the back curve for realism.\n\nThen I shift to the neck and head: sizing a skull ellipsoid, positioning the head joint tilted forward-down, and chaining neck joints from the spine's front tip. From there I start laying out the trunk as a downward-curving chain of joints extending from the head's front face.\n\nPlacing flattened torus rings along the trunk tangent to create wrinkle ridges...\n\nI'll pull at.at and at.bone directly from the ChainPoint, keeping ring geometry rigid per bone for the rest pose. For performance I'll keep torus tessellation low with flat shading, and plan the trunk tip with small finger-like protrusions and dark nostril discs on an end cap.\n\nNow I'm designing the tusks: curved ivory cones rooted near the upper jaw beside the trunk, swept along a downward-forward curve with tapering radius, rigged to the head bone, and colored with a gray-brown sheath band near the root transitioning to yellowish ivory at the tip.\n\nNext I'm working on the ears: large African-style fan shapes about 1 meter tall extruded from an outline positioned behind the eyes, angled outward roughly 30 degrees, and I'm working out the extrusion axes so the ear faces front-to-back correctly.\n\nDefining the ear outline points...\n\nFor the ear bone, I'll parent earL/earR joints to the head at the attachment point, aiming outward so the bone drives a natural fan motion. Then for eyes, I'm placing small brown irises with lashes (elephants have long lashes) on the sides of the head.\n\nNow I'm working out the eye as a decal with amber iris, dark pupil, highlight, and wrinkled eyelid, plus lash spikes and a brow ridge. For the head itself I'm shaping a scaled skull sphere with forehead and temple bumps, cheeks, and a small wedge-shaped lower jaw hinged separately from the trunk.\n\nI'm clarifying how joint orientation uses local forward/up/left directions versus how part scale axes map to world space, aiming to keep ellipsoid scales (width/height/length) consistent in model space like the hippo example, so I'll stick to world-axis-aligned ellipsoids wherever possible.\n\nNow for the skin, I'm planning a wrinkled elephant-gray paint: mixing mottled gray noise as a base, darkening at cell-edge boundaries for wrinkle lines, adding horizontal wobble stripes for extra wrinkle texture, and blending a reddish-brown dust tint on the lower body and legs to mimic mud dust, while keeping the belly and inner legs lighter.\n\nI want this applied as a single shared \"SKIN\" paint across all body parts for continuity, plus a separate ring-patterned paint for the trunk that alternates between the base skin tone and a darker tone based on position along the trunk's length.\n\nFor the pose, I'm keeping it a neutral standing rest pose—legs as straight vertical pillars with front legs slightly apart, trunk hanging down—and now thinking through how to model the columnar legs.\n\nForelegs use a chain through shoulder, elbow, wrist, foot; hind legs through hip, knee, ankle with a bit of bend, built as catmull paths with four named points so the last one forms a proper tip joint. For dimensions, I'm placing feet at the ground with round pads and toenails, tapering leg radius from a wide thigh down to a narrower lower leg, and positioning front and hind leg x/z offsets along with the hip joint height on the spine.\n\nNow I'm adding sole pads and toenails to the feet — pale wedge-shaped nails arranged around the front arc of each foot (four or five per foot), positioned to protrude slightly like a real elephant's.\n\nI'm also layering a wrinkled ankle crease above the foot and knee ridges for texture, then shaping the hind legs with a forward-bent knee joint, and adding rump/thigh masses as hip spheres to fill out the hindquarters.\n\nShoulder spheres get attached to the leg's hip joint so they move rigidly together. For skinning, I'm working out the skeleton: a root \"hips\" joint, with the spine chain parented to it, trying to simplify where exactly the chain's first point and the hips root should sit relative to each other.\n\nStill defining spine chain structure...\n\nNow for the tail: starting at the rump, I'll curve it downward and backward through a few points, ending in a tuft of coarse dark spikes at the tip, tapering the radius from 0.08 down to 0.035 over its 1.1 meter length.\n\nMoving to the head, I'm placing the skull center around (0, 2.7, 1.95), using a scaled cranium sphere for the shape, then adding small forehead domes to capture the rounded, slightly indented brow typical of African features.\n\nWorking through facial masses now — a cheek/face sphere forms the trunk base, and I'm positioning the lower jaw and lip structure below the trunk joint, aiming the jaw forward to get the hanging, narrow shape right.\n\nI'm placing a small dark pink ellipsoid behind the trunk base for the mouth interior, with the lower jaw sphere covering its lower edge, and setting tusk base positions symmetrically where they emerge from either side of the trunk root. The head proportions look right for an African elephant, so now I'm moving on to deciding the trunk root placement.\n\nNow I'm plotting the trunk's curve downward from the root, defining a series of points that sweep forward and taper toward the tip, making sure the tip hovers just above ground level rather than clipping through the floor given its radius.\n\nI'm also setting up a tapering radius array across trunk segments, using a 10-sided cross-section for a wide, flat-bellied trunk, and planning a small tilted cap at the tip with nostril-like details for realism.\n\nI'll use the same radius function for both the sweep and the rings so they align consistently, and keep the torus geometry low-poly with a small tube radius so the ridges protrude subtly along the trunk.\n\nFor color, I'm leaning toward a darker flat tone for continuity with the skin paint, applying it through the trunk paint object since the torus UVs just need position and normal data. I'll reuse this same ring technique for ankle wrinkles and a few body creases, mostly handled through paint with a couple of flattened torus folds near the leg tops.\n\nNow I'm designing the eyes as an SVG decal: an almond shape with wrinkly dark eyelid rings, an amber-brown eyeball with dark pupil and highlight, and top lashes, placed on the head surface. I'm working out the azimuth and elevation angles to position each eye symmetrically around the head center using the cranium, face, and crown surfaces.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the texture roster, aiming for 5-8 kinds: eye decal, and maybe skin wrinkle cards -- flat transparent cards with dark crease lines scattered across the body to mimic elephant skin cracks, seated flush against the surface via scatter with flow-aware orientation.\n\nFor the wrinkle cards specifically, I'm settling on 150-250 small cards around 0.2m, oriented so the crease lines follow the body's surface flow -- deciding between horizontal folds versus circumferential rings to best mimic real elephant skin texture, with some random spin added for variety.\n\nCard size bumping to 0.3x0.3, with strokes drawn solid dark brown-gray (not grey-tinted) at thick enough width to survive the alphaTest cutoff. I also want to add sparse bristly hair tufts on the crown, tail, and back for extra realism.\n\nBesides bristle cards (around 60), I'm planning a couple of textures (crease, bristle) plus several procedural paints: skin, trunk, ear, tusk (ivory with directional grain and darker tip), and toenail. I'll skip extra decals like mud-splashes or tusk cracks to keep things manageable, though I'm considering whether the ear should use a vein paint instead of a plain tint.\n\nFor positioning, I'm placing the foot cylinder so its base sits right at ground level (y=0) with toenails just above the floor, and defining a reusable ellipsoid helper function with low-poly sphere geometry and flat shading for the stylized, faceted look across most body parts.\n\nWriting faceted geometry logic...\n\nI'm adjusting the belly clearance and shifting the body shape slightly downward so the shoulder height stays around 3.1-3.3 while giving a natural underside gap. I'm also adding a slight concave dip to the spine profile between rump and shoulder to make the back line look more anatomically correct.\n\nNow I'm setting up the spine as a five-point chain running from tail to chest, using named joints so legs can attach properly: front legs anchor to the shoulder joint and hind legs to the hip joint, with positions spaced along the z-axis to match rump, hip, mid-back, shoulder, and chest locations.\n\nI'm placing the head on its own joint, oriented downward along the neck, and starting to define the cranium's position and scale within the head's local coordinate space.\n\nStill placing cheek sacs, temporal gland streaks, and checking eye socket positioning against the cranium's curved surface geometry.\n\nSince I can't actually run and verify things, I should write the code carefully and lean on known-safe patterns rather than risky API guesses. I'll check other samples like giraffe or redPanda that use cards, decal, along, and ring together to confirm the correct usage pattern before writing mine.\n\n",
